ur current recommendation and include evidence.\n\nWe have our own notes: B: very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills, makes nasty remarks, grumpy, uncooperative, weak memory for numbers. D: takes responsibility seriously, solves problems extremely well, concentrates very well, responds adequately to unexpected events, hot temper, know-it-all, weak leadership. A: anticipate dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities, regarded as show-off, not open to new ideas. C: 100% reliable, creates positive atmosphere, makes correct decisions quickly, keeps calm in crisis, understands complicated technology, difficulty communicating ideas, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\nWe need to consider criteria: reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention/accuracy, crew cooperation, professional communication, responsibility/maturity, adaptability/feedback.\n\nAnna's argument: C's reliability, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisions outweigh communication issues. She acknowledges concerns but says less detrimental than D's temperament.\n\nWe can respond: maybe argue that C's egocentric and unwillingness to further education are serious for long-haul, where continuous learning and adaptability are crucial. Also his communication difficulties could affect crew coordination. Meanwhile D's responsibility and problem solving are critical, and his hot temper may be manageable with training. Also B's stress handling and technical competence are strong, and his uncooperative behavior could be mitigated. We need to pick a candidate.\n\nWe could shift back to D, or propose B. Let's evaluate: D has strong responsibility, problem solving, concentration, but hot temper and arrogance. C has reliability and crew atmosphere but communication and egocentric, not willing to further education. Which is more critical? For long-haul,