icism and being unorganized could be addressed with training. So we could recommend A.\n\nAlternatively, we could keep C, but we already argued for C earlier. Now after discussion of D and A, we might reconsider. The panel is leaning D, but we could shift to A to bring new perspective. The guidelines: we should aim to converge on best candidate. We need to consider all criteria. Let's evaluate each candidate quickly:\n\n- A: strong technical competence (anticipates danger, complex connections, spatial vision), leadership, but concerns: not good at criticism, unorganized, show\u2011off, not open to new ideas, unfriendly, unhealthy. Lacks evidence on reliability, stress resilience, crew cooperation.\n\n- B: conscientious, handles stress well, good weather assessment, computer skills, but grumpy, uncooperative, no numeric memory data.\n\n- C: 100% reliable, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisions, but communication difficulty, egocentric, not willing to further education.\n\n- D: responsibility, problem solving, concentration, handles unexpected events, but hot temper, arrogance, weak leadership.\n\nWhich is best? Hard. We need to decide. Perhaps D's responsibility and problem solving are crucial, but his hot temper may be dangerous. C's reliability and crew atmosphere are strong, but communication issues and lack of development may be problematic. A's technical competence is high, but crew cooperation concerns. B's stress handling is good but uncooperative.\n\nGiven the safety-critical nature, reliability and crew cooperation are paramount. C is 100% reliable and creates positive atmosphere, which is strong. His communication difficulty and egocentric may be mitigated. D's temperament may be more risky. So perhaps C is best. Anna originally voted C. Now after discussion, we could reaffirm C, but we need to address A's new info. We can say that while A has strong technical skills, his lack of reliability evidence and crew cooperation concerns make him less suitable than C's proven reliability and positive crew atmosphere. So we keep C.\n\nThus Anna will reaffirm C, referencing A's concerns and D's temperament. Provide evidence: C 100% reliable, positive crew atmosphere, quick correct decisions. A's unfriendly, not open to new ideas, unhealthy habits, lack of reliability evidence. D's hot temper, weak leadership. So C remains best.\n\nThus METADATA vote Candidate C.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE: \"I remain convinced that Candidate C is the best...\" referencing prior discussion, evidence, and why A and D are less suitable.\n\nProceed."}
